  <!-- VANCOMYCIN SECTION -->
  <section id="vancomycin" class="section print-friendly">
    <h2 class="section-title">VANCOMYCIN</h2>

    <div class="subsection">
      <h3 class="subsection-title">Critical Facts</h3>
      <div class="fact-box danger">
        <div class="fact-label">Red Man Syndrome</div>
        <div class="fact-content">Infusion-related reaction (NOT true allergy) → facial flushing, erythema, pruritus. Prevent: slow infusion (≥60 min), pretreat with diphenhydramine + hydrocortisone.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="fact-box danger">
        <div class="fact-label">Monitoring: TROUGH Levels</div>
        <div class="fact-content">NOT peak levels. Draw 30 min before 4th/5th dose. Target: 15-20 mcg/mL. Narrow therapeutic index.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="fact-box danger">
        <div class="fact-label">Irreversible Ototoxicity</div>
        <div class="fact-content">Hearing loss + tinnitus. Risk increases: high doses, renal impairment, concurrent ototoxic drugs (aminoglycosides). Once damaged = permanent.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="fact-box danger">
        <div class="fact-label">Nephrotoxicity + Synergistic Risk</div>
        <div class="fact-content">Monitor Cr/BUN. Vancomycin + gentamicin = DANGEROUS synergistic kidney AND hearing damage.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="fact-box warning">
        <div class="fact-label">CNS Penetration</div>
        <div class="fact-content">Only 15-30% even with inflamed meninges. For meningitis: require HIGHER trough levels (often 20+ mcg/mL), not standard dosing.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="subsection">
      <h3 class="subsection-title">Vancomycin vs Alternatives</h3>
      <table>
        <tr>
          <th>Drug</th>
          <th>Route</th>
          <th>Monitoring</th>
          <th>CNS Penetration</th>
          <th>Key Concern</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="drug-name">Vancomycin</span></td>
          <td>IV only</td>
          <td>Trough levels</td>
          <td>Poor (15-30%)</td>
          <td>Ototoxicity, nephrotoxicity</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="drug-name">Linezolid</span></td>
          <td>Oral or IV</td>
          <td>None required</td>
          <td>Excellent</td>
          <td>Thrombocytopenia, expensive</td>
        </tr>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ANTIBIOTICS SECTION -->
  <section id="antibiotics" class="section print-friendly">
    <h2 class="section-title">ANTIBIOTICS: CRITICAL DIFFERENCES</h2>

    <div class="subsection">
      <h3 class="subsection-title">Age-Based Contraindications</h3>
      <table>
        <tr>
          <th>Drug Class</th>
          <th>Contraindication</th>
          <th>Why</th>
          <th>Exception</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="critical">Fluoroquinolones</span><br>(Cipro, Levofloxacin)</td>
          <td><span class="critical">&lt;18 years</span><br><span class="critical">&gt;60 years</span></td>
          <td>Arthropathy, cartilage damage (children); tendon rupture, especially Achilles (elderly)</td>
          <td>Peds CF patients (Pseudomonas severity)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="critical">Tetracyclines</span><br>(Doxycycline, Minocycline)</td>
          <td><span class="critical">&lt;8 years</span><br><span class="critical">Pregnancy</span></td>
          <td>Permanent tooth discoloration, enamel dysplasia; teratogenic, fetal bone/tooth effects</td>
          <td>None in these populations</td>
        </tr>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="subsection">
      <h3 class="subsection-title">Tetracyclines: Additional Considerations</h3>
      <ul class="quick-list">
        <li><span class="caution">Photosensitivity:</span> Severe sun sensitivity - patient MUST avoid exposure</li>
        <li><span class="caution">Dairy/Calcium:</span> Chelation complex formation → dramatically reduced absorption</li>
        <li><span class="safe">Off-label acne/rosacea:</span> Anti-inflammatory effect (NOT antibacterial)</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="subsection">
      <h3 class="subsection-title">Unique Coverage & Special Uses</h3>
      <table>
        <tr>
          <th>Drug</th>
          <th>Unique Feature</th>
          <th>Critical Pearl</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="drug-name">Metronidazole</span></td>
          <td>Only antibiotic covering BOTH anaerobes AND protozoa</td>
          <td><span class="critical">AVOID ALL ALCOHOL</span> (48h after) = disulfiram-like reaction</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="drug-name">Macrolides</span><br>(Azithromycin)</td>
          <td>Coverage: Legionella, Listeria, Mycoplasma</td>
          <td><span class="critical">QT PROLONGATION risk</span> → arrhythmias. CYP3A4 inhibition.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="drug-name">Carbapenems</span><br>(Imipenem)</td>
          <td>BBB penetration for CNS infections</td>
          <td><span class="critical">SEIZURE risk</span> (esp. imipenem). Cilastatin prevents renal inactivation.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="drug-name">Doxycycline</span></td>
          <td>Anti-inflammatory (non-antibiotic use)</td>
          <td>Acne, rosacea, possible gastroparesis (prokinetic)</td>
        </tr>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="subsection">
      <h3 class="subsection-title">QT Prolongation Alert</h3>
      <div class="fact-box danger">
        <div class="fact-label">Macrolides + Fluoroquinolones</div>
        <div class="fact-content"><span class="critical">BOTH carry QT prolongation risk</span> → Torsades de Pointes → sudden cardiac death. Higher risk: elderly, electrolyte abnormalities (↓K, ↓Mg), other QT drugs, baseline long QT syndrome. Monitor continuously.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- HIV/AIDS SECTION -->
  <section id="hiv" class="section print-friendly">
    <h2 class="section-title">HIV & AIDS</h2>

    <div class="subsection">
      <h3 class="subsection-title">CD4+ T Cells: The Core Concept</h3>
      <div class="fact-box">
        <div class="fact-label">Why CD4 Matters</div>
        <div class="fact-content">HIV targets CD4+ T helper cells (immune system coordinators). When destroyed → entire immune response uncoordinated/ineffective. <span class="critical">CD4 &lt;200 = severe immunosuppression; CD4 &lt;50 = critical</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="subsection">
      <h3 class="subsection-title">HIV Disease Stages</h3>
      <table>
        <tr>
          <th>Stage</th>
          <th>CD4 Count</th>
          <th>Viral Load</th>
          <th>Clinical Features</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Acute Infection</strong></td>
          <td>Variable</td>
          <td><span class="critical">Very High</span> (millions)</td>
          <td>Fever, rash, malaise (mono-like); often unrecognized</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Chronic Asymptomatic</strong></td>
          <td>Gradual decline (200-800)</td>
          <td>Moderate-High</td>
          <td>Few/no symptoms despite CD4 decline</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>AIDS (Stage 3)</strong></td>
          <td><span class="critical">&lt;200 or AIDS-defining illness</span></td>
          <td>Very High</td>
          <td>OIs, malignancies, CNS symptoms</td>
        </tr>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="subsection">
      <h3 class="subsection-title">CD4 Thresholds for Opportunistic Infections</h3>
      <table>
        <tr>
          <th>CD4 Threshold</th>
          <th>Opportunistic Infections</th>
          <th>Prophylaxis/Action</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="critical">&lt;200</span></td>
          <td>PCP (Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia)</td>
          <td>Start TMP-SMX prophylaxis</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="critical">&lt;100</span></td>
          <td>Toxoplasmosis, Cryptococcosis</td>
          <td>Add toxo prophylaxis; add fluconazole for cryptococcus</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="critical">&lt;50</span></td>
          <td>MAC, CMV, Histoplasmosis</td>
          <td>Add azithromycin (MAC), ganciclovir/valganciclovir (CMV)</td>
        </tr>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="subsection">
      <h3 class="subsection-title">Important Concepts</h3>
      <div class="grid-2">
        <div class="fact-box">
          <div class="fact-label">Prophylaxis Discontinuation</div>
          <div class="fact-content">When CD4 recovers &gt;100-200 on ART for 3+ months, OI prophylaxis can be STOPPED. Sign of immune reconstitution.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="fact-box">
          <div class="fact-label">HIV-Associated Dementia</div>
          <div class="fact-content">HIV directly crosses BBB, infects brain tissue. Progressive cognitive decline. More common with CD4 &lt;50. Partially reversible with ART.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="fact-box">
          <div class="fact-label">U=U: Undetectable = Untransmittable</div>
          <div class="fact-content"><span class="safe">Viral load &lt;50 copies/mL = ZERO sexual transmission risk.</span> Requires perfect adherence and monitoring.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="subsection">
      <h3 class="subsection-title">AIDS-Defining Illnesses</h3>
      <ul class="quick-list">
        <li>Candidiasis (esophageal, bronchial, pulmonary)</li>
        <li>CMV (retinitis, esophagitis, colitis, encephalitis)</li>
        <li>Cryptococcosis (pulmonary, CNS)</li>
        <li>Cryptosporidiosis (chronic intestinal)</li>
        <li>Toxoplasmosis (CNS)</li>
        <li>Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia (PCP)</li>
        <li>Mycobacterium avium complex (MAC)</li>
        <li>Tuberculosis (pulmonary or disseminated)</li>
        <li>Histoplasmosis</li>
        <li>HIV-Associated Dementia</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ANTIVIRALS SECTION -->
  <section id="antivirals" class="section print-friendly">
    <h2 class="section-title">ANTIVIRALS</h2>

    <div class="subsection">
      <h3 class="subsection-title">Acyclovir & Herpes Antivirals</h3>
      <table>
        <tr>
          <th>Drug</th>
          <th>Mechanism</th>
          <th>Key Limitation</th>
          <th>Clinical Use</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="drug-name">Acyclovir</span></td>
          <td>Inhibits viral DNA polymerase during replication</td>
          <td><span class="critical">Does NOT eliminate dormant virus</span> in nerve ganglia</td>
          <td>Active infection only; suppressive therapy reduces recurrence 50-70%</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="drug-name">Valacyclovir</span></td>
          <td>Acyclovir prodrug (better bioavailability)</td>
          <td>Same limitation as acyclovir</td>
          <td>Oral dosing; better absorption</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="drug-name">Ganciclovir</span></td>
          <td>Inhibits CMV DNA polymerase</td>
          <td><span class="critical">BLACK BOX: bone marrow suppression, infertility</span></td>
          <td>CMV retinitis, esophagitis, colitis (severe CD4 &lt;50)</td>
        </tr>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="subsection">
      <h3 class="subsection-title">Oseltamivir (Tamiflu) - Influenza</h3>
      <div class="fact-box warning">
        <div class="fact-label">Critical Timing</div>
        <div class="fact-content">MUST be started within <span class="critical">48 hours of symptom onset</span> to be effective. After 48h = minimal benefit.</div>
      </div>
      <ul class="quick-list">
        <li>Neuraminidase inhibitor → prevents viral release from infected cells</li>
        <li>Reduces symptom duration if started early</li>
        <li>Does NOT prevent transmission</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="subsection">
      <h3 class="subsection-title">Antiviral Summary Table</h3>
      <table>
        <tr>
          <th>Drug</th>
          <th>Target Virus</th>
          <th>Route</th>
          <th>Renal Monitoring</th>
          <th>Key Warnings</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="drug-name">Acyclovir</span></td>
          <td>HSV, VZV</td>
          <td>IV, PO</td>
          <td>Yes (renal clearance)</td>
          <td>Neurotoxicity at high doses, dehydration risk</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="drug-name">Valacyclovir</span></td>
          <td>HSV, VZV, EBV</td>
          <td>PO</td>
          <td>Yes</td>
          <td>Thrombotic microangiopathy in immunocompromised</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="drug-name">Ganciclovir</span></td>
          <td>CMV</td>
          <td>IV (valganciclovir PO)</td>
          <td>Yes (critical)</td>
          <td><span class="critical">Bone marrow suppression, infertility, teratogen</span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="drug-name">Oseltamivir</span></td>
          <td>Influenza</td>
          <td>PO</td>
          <td>Yes if renal impairment</td>
          <td><span class="critical">Must start &lt;48h symptom onset</span>; neuropsych effects rare</td>
        </tr>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="subsection">
      <h3 class="subsection-title">Antiviral Resistance</h3>
      <div class="fact-box danger">
        <div class="fact-label">Prevention Strategy</div>
        <div class="fact-content">Poor adherence → viral replication with suboptimal drug levels → resistance development. Emphasize: <span class="critical">PERFECT adherence required</span> for ART and all antivirals.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

